 ## Overview
-Dynamic Particle Distribution is a particle-based sampling method for isosurface mesh generation that uses inter-particle forces to drive particles to local energy minima, controlling density via a sizing field $h$ and correcting for Euclidean distance approximation artifacts using a contraction factor $g$ [S1]. It builds on [Point Engine for Scalar Fields](7529-point-engine-for-scalar-fields.md) by taking the initial vertex positions and refining them into a uniformly distributed point cloud suitable for high-quality triangulation, rather than outputting a mesh directly [S1]. Unlike Marching Cubes variants which produce fixed topological structures, this method adapts particle density to the local curvature and feature size of the implicit surface [S1].
+Dynamic Particle Distribution is a particle-based sampling method for isosurface mesh generation that uses inter-particle forces to drive particles to local energy minima, controlling density via a sizing field $h$ and correcting for Euclidean distance approximation artifacts using a contraction factor $g$ [S1]. It builds on [Point Engine for Scalar Fields](7529-point-engine-for-scalar-fields.md) by taking initial vertex positions and refining them into a uniformly distributed point cloud suitable for high-quality triangulation, rather than outputting a mesh directly [S1]. Unlike Marching Cubes variants which produce fixed topological structures, this method adapts particle density to the local curvature and feature size of the implicit surface [S1].
 
